# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# The interface org.velarx.Storage: a storage driver that is a program of its
# own, asked by the engine for the content of its images and the root file
# systems of its containers.  The driver owns where the bytes live and how a
# root is made of them; the engine owns the network, the table of contents and
# tar-split of each layer, the identities (diff ids, EROFS digests, owners) and
# the reachability of everything.  The pull is driven by the driver through
# org.velarx.StorageFeed (BeginPull).  Generated from the Rust types
# (crates/storage-client/src/proto.rs and interface.rs) by the test
# `interface::tests::the_interface_file_is_current`:
# VELARX_WRITE_INTERFACES=1 cargo test -p velarx-storage-client --lib interface
# writes it.  docs/STORAGE.md maps it to the StorageDriver trait.
#
# Versions: the engine calls GetInfo first on each connection and refuses a
# `proto` other than its own (1); there is no negotiation and no
# compatibility.  A change of a shape is a new version, so what is announced for
# later is a capability (`later` in GetInfo), not a new shape.  Unknown fields
# are ignored.  A value of an enum the engine does not know, in a reply, is a
# Storage error that names it, not a protocol error; the engine asks only for
# what it can take (it asks `idmap_mount: true` only when it can handle `mount`).
# What the engine learns to do within a version is an optional field of
# `capabilities` (absent: false), `objects` so far (HasObjects, and the `copy`
# events of the feed): the engine asks and sends only what the driver
# announces, and a driver that announces what an older engine does not know is
# asked nothing of it.
#
# One connection for each call, from the engine's user or root; the engine
# talks only to a driver served by its own user or root (SO_PEERCRED).  A
# connection with no reply for `[storage.varlink] timeout_secs`, a connect not
# accepted and a request not read within it, are failed (TimedOut).  A call
# that can take longer (CreateRoot, CreateImageVolume, BeginPull, Collect,
# Repair, Check) is made with `more`, and the driver sends a `progress` reply at
# least every timeout_secs / 2; every reply starts the deadline again.
#
# Limits (GetInfo `limits`, applied by the engine, which splits a request that
# would exceed one and never sends it): a message is at most 1048576 bytes of JSON and
# carries at most 16 descriptors; a Fetch asks for at most 4096 objects, a ReadObjects
# for at most 4096 ranges (the ranges are answered in the order asked, across calls).
# What can be longer than a message does not travel in one: the keep set of
# Collect is a sealed memory file of JSON lines, and its length is the argument.
#
# Descriptors are named, not counted: a reply that carries descriptors says for
# each which one it is by its position in the reply (Mounted.fd).  A position used
# twice, outside the reply, or a descriptor no mount names, is a refused reply.
# The driver makes a new mount for each reply and closes every copy of it after
# sending it; the monitor attaches a clone of it.
#
# Every call but GetInfo and ListStores takes the `store`, the id of the store the
# engine keeps in a file of its graph root (32 hex digits); a driver isolates
# stores by the pair (uid of the peer, store).
#
# Roots: a `directory` root is made beneath the `workdir` of CreateRoot (the
# work directory of the store, the engine's) and named relative to it; any
# other path is refused, and the monitor resolves it beneath that base without
# following a link.  Every other root is a `mount_fd`.
#
# Errors: Missing and Corrupt carry a `remedy` (pull_again, repair,
# recreate_container); Unsupported, NotFound, Busy, Unavailable and Failed carry a
# reason.  An error name the engine does not know is a Storage error that carries
# the name in its message.  The engine makes Unavailable (connect refused, the
# socket gone: start the storage service), TimedOut and ProtocolMismatch (both
# versions named: upgrade the driver or velarx) of a transport that failed; a driver
# whose own backend does not answer sends Unavailable.  Contradicted,
# DigestEnforced and Refused are verdicts of the engine, and a driver never sends
# them.
#
# Lazy images: the driver sees none.  An image pulled with --lazy is staged and
# run by the engine from its own files (the table of contents of each layer is
# the engine's), and when its files are all there the engine pulls its layers
# into the driver as an eager pull does (BeginPull, Index, Fetch, the read-back
# of ReadObjects, Commit).  An image with a layer that cannot be served that way
# is refused by the engine before anything is staged.  `capabilities.lazy` is
# the engine's word for itself, not a driver's; what a driver says is ignored.
#
# Idempotence: CreateRoot, CreateImageVolume, RemoveRoot, RemoveImage, Repair,
# Collect, Commit, Abort and DropStore can be called again with the same arguments
# and give the same state.  Mounts is asked at every start and MountImage at every
# read of an image, and both give new descriptors each time.  The engine does
# not call Collect while a create, a removal or a pull of the same store is
# between its first call and the record of its result.

interface org.velarx.Storage

# A layer as the engine names it to the driver: the key the driver stores it
# under, the diff id of its image's configuration, and its OCI chain id
# (computed by the engine; a driver whose layers are not chained ignores it).
type LayerRef (
	# `sha256:HEX` (a layer pulled whole) or `toc:sha256:HEX` (through its
	# table of contents); `sha512:` in place of `sha256:` for an image of
	# that algorithm
	key: string,
	# The diff id of the layer, `sha256:HEX` or `sha512:HEX`
	diff_id: string,
	# The OCI ChainID of the layer as the top of its stack
	chain_id: string
)

# A layer of the image a pull is for: all of the image's layers, in order.
type PullLayer (
	key: string,
	diff_id: string,
	chain_id: string,
	# The chain id of the layer below it, none for the first
	parent: ?string,
	# The driver has the layer (`HasLayers` said so for this place of the
	# stack): it is in the list so that every layer knows its parent, and
	# nothing of it is fetched
	stored: bool
)

# A range of the bytes of one stored object.
type Range (
	# The key of the object, as the table of contents of a layer names it
	# (its fs-verity digest)
	key: string,
	# The first byte
	offset: int,
	# How many bytes: the object must have them all
	length: int
)

# A range of ids: the ids from `container_id` of the container are those
# from `host_id` on the host.
type IdMap (container_id: int, host_id: int, size: int)

# The user namespace of the container.
type Mapping (
	uid: []IdMap,
	gid: []IdMap,
	# An owner no range maps is shown as the root of the container (else
	# the image does not start)
	squash: bool
)

# The uid and gid, as the host sees them, of the root of the container's
# user namespace.
type Owner (uid: int, gid: int)

# The fs-verity digests the engine accepts for the image of one layer.
type Accepted (digests: []string)

# What the engine asks of a mount, the same for a root and for an image
# mounted in the container.
type MountAsk (
	# The user namespace of the container, none: the ids of the engine
	mapping: ?Mapping,
	# The monitor can put the mapping on the mount (the engine is root of
	# the initial user namespace): the driver may answer `idmap: mount`.
	# False: it never does
	idmap_mount: bool,
	# The SELinux context of the mount of the container
	label: ?string,
	# The digests the engine accepts for the image of each layer, in order
	# (the digests are enforced): a driver that cannot check them answers
	# `Unsupported`
	verity: ?[]Accepted
)

# An image mount of the container: its index among the `--mount
# type=image` of the container, counted from 0, and what is asked of it.
type VolumeAsk (index: int, ask: MountAsk)

# What a mount is.
type MountKind (
	# A directory that the driver made for the container beneath the work
	# directory of the store: `path`.  A root only
	directory,
	# A detached mount (`open_tree(OPEN_TREE_CLONE)` or `fsmount`), one
	# mount, its root: the descriptor `fd` of the reply
	mount_fd
)

# Who put the container's id mapping on the mount.
type Idmap (
	# No mapping was asked
	none,
	# The driver delivers the container's ids itself (the copy was made
	# with them, a server translates them, its lowers are idmapped)
	applied,
	# The mount carries the ids of the image, and the monitor puts the
	# mapping on the clone it attaches; only when `idmap_mount` was asked
	mount
)

# Whether the SELinux label that was asked is on the mount.
type Label (
	# The driver did nothing about it (or none was asked): the engine
	# labels the files of a root, and refuses an image mount, when one was
	# asked
	none,
	# The mount carries it as its `context=`
	mount_context,
	# The driver labelled the files as it made them
	files
)

# What a mount is, as the driver says it.
type Mounted (
	kind: MountKind,
	# With `mount_fd` in the reply of `Mounts`: the position of the
	# descriptor among those of the reply.  Every mount that has one says
	# which it is; a position used twice, outside the reply, or a
	# descriptor no mount names, is a refused reply.  Never with a
	# `CreateRoot` or `CreateImageVolume`, which hand over none
	fd: ?int,
	# With `directory`: the path of the directory relative to the `workdir`
	# of `CreateRoot` (plain names: no `/` first, no `.`, `..` or empty
	# one).  The work directory is the base the monitor checks and opens
	# without following a link, and the directory is resolved beneath it:
	# a root is never anywhere else on the host
	path: ?string,
	idmap: Idmap,
	label: Label
)

# How a container with a user namespace gets the layers owned by its ids.
type IdmapWay (
	# One mount whose files carry the ids of the image, the monitor puts
	# the mapping on the clone it attaches (needs root of the initial user
	# namespace)
	monitor_mount,
	# The driver idmaps its own mounts
	driver_mount,
	# The ids are translated as the files are copied for the container
	at_copy,
	# A file system server translates them
	translate
)

# What a driver does for one purpose of a mount.
type MountCaps (
	# How user namespaces are supported, in the order the driver prefers;
	# empty: a mapping is not supported
	idmap: []IdmapWay
)

# The mounts a driver makes: how the root of a container and the images
# mounted in it get their owners.
type MountWays (
	# The root file system of a container
	root: MountCaps,
	# The images mounted in a container; none: no image mounts
	volume: ?MountCaps
)

# What a long call tells while it goes.
type Progress (
	# What the driver is at, for the person who waits
	message: ?string,
	done: ?int,
	total: ?int
)

# What a reply of `BeginPull` is.
type PullEventKind (
	# The pull goes on (`continues`); sent at least every `timeout_secs / 2`,
	# also while the driver's own calls to the feed run
	progress,
	# Every object the driver needs is staged: the last reply
	staged
)

# What a check found wrong with what a driver made of an image.
type ProblemKind (artifact_missing, artifact_unreadable, artifact_writable, artifact_id_mismatch, artifact_differs, artifact_seal_mismatch, annotation_missing, annotation_mismatch, identity_missing, toc_missing, toc_differs, object_missing, object_differs)

# A problem a check found.
type Problem (
	kind: ProblemKind,
	message: string,
	# The layer it is about
	layer: ?string,
	# The file it is about, when the driver has one to name
	path: ?string,
	# Making the artifacts again mends it
	repair: bool
)

# What a driver made of a layer, for the engine to record.
type Artifact (
	# The layer
	key: string,
	# The digest the driver's image of the layer has (composefs: the
	# fs-verity digest of its EROFS image); the engine records it as the
	# identity of what the driver made
	image_digest: string,
	owners: Owners
)

# The users and groups that own the files of a layer.
type Owners (uids: []int, gids: []int)

# A name and a value the driver reports of itself.
type Pair (name: string, value: string)

# A store the driver holds.
type StoreEntry (
	# The id of the store: 32 hex digits
	store: string,
	# What it takes on the driver's disk
	bytes: int,
	# When an engine last opened it (seconds since the epoch), none if the
	# driver does not know
	last_open: ?int
)

# What mends an artifact that is missing or wrong.
type Remedy (
	# The image is pulled again
	pull_again,
	# The artifacts are made again from what the driver keeps (`Repair`)
	repair,
	# The container is removed and created again: what it wrote in its
	# writable layer is lost with it
	recreate_container
)

# Who the driver is and what it does.  The first call of every
# connection: the engine refuses a `proto` other than its own.
method GetInfo() -> (
	# What the driver calls itself, for the logs and the messages
	name: string,
	# The version of the driver (its package's)
	version: string,
	# The version of the protocol: the engine refuses another one
	proto: int,
	# Words; a client ignores those it does not know
	capabilities: []string,
	# Named limits; a client ignores those it does not know
	limits: [string]int,
	mounts: MountWays
)

# Which of `layers` the driver has: one answer for each, in the order
# asked (at most `max_layers` of the capabilities), true for a layer
# that is committed and complete.  A layer that is only staged, or
# committed with another diff id, is not reported.  A chained driver
# answers for the place of each layer in its stack.
method HasLayers(store: string, layers: []LayerRef) -> (
	# One for each layer asked, in the order asked (the same layer at two
	# places of a stack is two questions): true for a layer that is
	# committed and complete
	have: []bool
)

# Which of `objects` the driver holds: one answer for each, in the
# order asked (at most `limits.wants` of them), true for an object
# it has committed and can read now, under that key or by an alias
# of it (the hash of its content, for an object it names by its
# fs-verity digest).  An object that is only staged by a pull, or
# that a collection removed, is not held.  Answered from the driver's
# own store.  Asked only of a driver that says `objects` in its
# capabilities; the engine names a held object as the source of a
# `copy` event of a `Fetch`.
method HasObjects(store: string, objects: []string) -> (
	# One for each object asked, in the order asked: true for an object the
	# driver has committed and can read now
	have: []bool
)

# The pull of the layers of an image, driven by the driver.  `layers`
# is the whole ordered list of the image, those `stored` included.
# The call carries the descriptor 0: the driver's end of a socket
# pair on which the engine serves `org.velarx.StorageFeed` for this
# pull only.  Called with `more`, it lives as long as the pull: the
# driver replies `progress` at least every `timeout_secs / 2`, also
# while its own calls to the feed run, and `staged` once every object
# it needs is staged (the engine keeps the feed open after it until it
# has committed or aborted the pull: org.velarx.StorageFeed, "The
# end").  Closing the connection cancels the pull.  The
# `pull` is a random id the engine made for this attempt (32 hex
# digits): a call with the id of a pull the driver has resumes it, an
# id it does not know is a new pull, and the engine reuses an id only
# to resume its own.
method BeginPull(store: string, pull: string, layers: []PullLayer) -> (kind: PullEventKind, progress: ?Progress)

# The bytes of objects, committed or staged by `pull`, in pipe form.
# The call carries the descriptor 0: the write end of a pipe.  Called
# with `more`: one reply for each range, in the order asked, and the
# reply for range `i` is sent **before** the `length` bytes of its
# range are written to the pipe (a range larger than the pipe buffer
# would block the driver, and the engine too, were the bytes first).
# At most `limits.ranges` ranges.  A key that is no object's name is
# `Missing` with the remedy `pull_again`; an object with fewer bytes
# than a range wants is `Corrupt`.  The engine checks what it needs
# of the bytes: a driver is trusted to store, not to be right.
method ReadObjects(store: string, pull: ?string, ranges: []Range) -> (
	# The index of the range among those asked: in the order asked
	index: int,
	# How many bytes the object has (a driver that tells it lets the engine
	# see an object longer than its index)
	size: ?int
)

# Publish the layers of the pull and their objects, all or nothing,
# with one barrier.  Idempotent: succeeds when each layer is
# committed by this pull or already committed, by anyone, with the
# same diff id; `Busy` with another.
method Commit(store: string, pull: string, layers: []LayerRef) -> ()

# End the pull: drop only what no other live pull and no committed
# layer uses.  Idempotent.
method Abort(store: string, pull: string) -> ()

# Make what the driver keeps for the root of a container, and say what
# the root will be: the reply has the kind of the mount, the mapping
# and the label it will have, and **no descriptor** (none without an
# image, `--rootfs`).  Every later `Mounts` answers the same kind,
# `idmap` and `label`.  `workdir` is the work directory of the store,
# a directory of the engine's user closed to others: a `directory`
# root is made beneath it and named relative to it, and a driver
# that answers `mount_fd` may ignore it.  Idempotent.  Called with `more`: a call that
# costs O(image) for a driver that copies sends `progress` at least
# every `timeout_secs / 2`.  `Busy` for a second `CreateRoot` of one
# container that runs.
method CreateRoot(store: string, container: string, workdir: string, image: ?string, layers: []LayerRef, owner: ?Owner, ask: MountAsk, size_limit: ?int) -> (
	progress: ?Progress,
	# In the last reply: what the root will be, with no descriptor (none
	# without an image)
	root: ?Mounted
)

# The same for the image mounted in a container as its `--mount
# type=image` number `index`.  Idempotent; `progress` as `CreateRoot`.
# The engine counts the volumes that are `mount_fd` and refuses more
# than 15 at create.
method CreateImageVolume(store: string, container: string, index: int, image: string, layers: []LayerRef, ask: MountAsk) -> (
	progress: ?Progress,
	# In the last reply: what the volume will be, with no descriptor
	volume: ?Mounted
)

# The root file system of a container and the images mounted in it,
# as the monitor mounts them for this start: **the only source of
# descriptors**.  Asked at every start, restart of the engine and
# restore, and gives new descriptors each time.  The mounts of one
# container per call.  The reply carries the descriptors of the
# `mount_fd` mounts, each named by its position among them
# (`Mounted.fd`).  The driver makes a **new** mount for each reply, a
# detached `open_tree(OPEN_TREE_CLONE)` or `fsmount`, closes every
# copy of it after sending it, and never hands the same mount twice.
# A volume that was never created is `Missing` with the remedy
# `recreate_container`.
method Mounts(store: string, container: string, root: ?MountAsk, volumes: []VolumeAsk) -> (
	# When it was asked
	root: ?Mounted,
	# In the order asked
	volumes: []Mounted
)

# The root file system of the image made of `layers` as its containers
# see it, as the one descriptor of the reply: a read-only mount of it (a detached
# `open_tree(OPEN_TREE_CLONE)` or `fsmount`) or, where the service
# cannot make one, a directory.  For the engine's reads of what a
# create needs of the image (the user's entries of `/etc/passwd` and
# `/etc/group`, `/etc/localtime`, the `--workdir`, the sub path of an
# image mount, the content of a volume that takes the image's): the
# engine opens every name below it with `openat2` (beneath, no link,
# no magic link, no other mount), reads regular files only and at
# most what it needs, refuses the root of the host, and closes the
# descriptor when it is done.  The mount is new for each reply and
# the service keeps no hold on it (as for `Mounts`); what it keeps
# to make one (a copy of the image, for a service that copies its
# layers) is its own, dropped by the `Collect` that no longer keeps
# a layer of it.  It carries the ids of the image, no mapping.  A
# layer the service does not have is `Missing` with the remedy
# `pull_again`.
method MountImage(store: string, layers: []LayerRef) -> ()

# The root of the writable layer of the container, as the one
# descriptor of the reply: a directory, or a detached clone of the
# mount the container runs on (the same superblock), never a second
# mount of the same upper.  For the engine's walks: the user files and
# the label at create, `cp` and `export` of a stopped container, and
# `diff`, `changes` and `commit`, which may be of a running one.
method WritableRoot(store: string, container: string) -> ()

# The bytes of the files the container wrote.
method Size(store: string, container: string) -> (
	# The bytes of the files the container wrote
	bytes: int
)

# Whether the container has a writable layer made.
method Exists(store: string, container: string) -> (exists: bool)

# Remove the writable layer of the container, its image volumes with
# it.  Idempotent.
method RemoveRoot(store: string, container: string) -> ()

# Forget what the driver keeps of a removed image (the layers go at a
# collection).  Idempotent.
method RemoveImage(store: string, image: string) -> ()

# What is wrong with what the driver made of the layers of `image`;
# `full` reads it and compares it with what the tables of contents
# make.  Called with `more`: `progress` as `CreateRoot`.
method Check(store: string, image: string, layers: []LayerRef, full: bool) -> (
	progress: ?Progress,
	# In the last reply
	problems: ?[]Problem
)

# Make the artifacts of the layers again from what the driver keeps,
# and answer their identities, which the engine checks against those
# it makes from the tables of contents before it records them.
# Idempotent.  Called with `more`: `progress` as `CreateRoot`.
method Repair(store: string, layers: []LayerRef) -> (
	progress: ?Progress,
	# In the last reply: made again from what the driver keeps, and
	# answered for the engine to check against the identities it makes from
	# the tables of contents
	artifacts: ?[]Artifact
)

# What the driver holds on its disk for itself: the files and bytes
# of its own artifacts (the objects are the engine's).
method Usage(store: string) -> (files: int, bytes: int)

# What the driver reports of itself, as names and values.
method Status(store: string) -> (pairs: []Pair)

# The stores of the caller's uid that the driver holds, for a driver
# that serves several: each with what it takes and when an engine last
# opened it, so that a store no engine opens any more is found (the
# id of the engine's store is in a file of its graph root).  A driver
# isolates stores by the pair (uid of the peer, store).
method ListStores() -> (stores: []StoreEntry)

# Remove the store and everything under it (layers, roots, image
# mounts, staging).  Idempotent.  `Busy` while a pull or a container
# of it is in progress; a name that is not a store id (32 hex digits)
# is refused before it names a path.
method DropStore(store: string) -> ()

# Remove what the driver holds that the keep set does not name, and
# what an interrupted pull left.  The keep set is the JSON lines of
# the descriptor 0, a sealed memory file of `keep_length` bytes (the
# grace period, then one record for each layer, chain, image,
# container and pull: a set can be larger than a message).  What the
# driver finds that nothing names it keeps while it has changed within
# the grace period.  Idempotent.  Called with `more`: `progress` as
# `CreateRoot`.
method Collect(store: string, keep_length: int) -> (
	progress: ?Progress,
	# In the last reply
	bytes_freed: ?int,
	# In the last reply: what could not be removed, one line each
	errors: []string
)

# An artifact is not there or not recorded; `remedy` mends it, and the
# message ends with it
error Missing (reason: string, layer: ?string, remedy: Remedy)

# An artifact is not the one recorded or cannot be read; `remedy` mends
# it (`recreate_container` loses what the container wrote in its
# writable layer, and the message says so)
error Corrupt (reason: string, layer: ?string, remedy: Remedy)

# The driver cannot do what was asked: a capability it lacks
error Unsupported (reason: string)

# Something the call names is not there: a pull, a container
error NotFound (reason: string)

# The call conflicts with one in progress (two `CreateRoot` of one
# container, a `Collect` during a `Commit` of another process): it is
# made again later
error Busy (reason: string)

# The host refuses the SELinux label asked for the files of an image
# mount: another label, or none, is asked
error LabelRefused (reason: string)

# The driver's own backend does not answer (an object store, a
# service): the storage service is started
error Unavailable (reason: string)

# The driver tried and failed
error Failed (reason: string)
